The Taylor-Green vortex: the verification problem for the incompressible
rungs (doc/incompressible.md Gates 4 and 6).

Compile.  ALWAYS 'make clean' when changing INIT= or DFMM=; the Makefile does
not treat either as a dependency of condinit.o, so a stale condinit.o will
silently run the PREVIOUS problem's initial condition and hand you a
plausible-looking wrong answer.

  rung 1, incompressible Navier-Stokes -- Gate 4, the exact-solution test:
    cd bin && make clean && make COMPILER=METAL NDIM=3 HYDRO=1 DFMM=0 INIT=TAYLORGREEN
    ... with incomp_stress='viscous'

  rung 4, incompressible dfmm -- Gate 6, the Chapman-Enskog limit:
    cd bin && make clean && make COMPILER=METAL NDIM=3 HYDRO=1 DFMM=4 INIT=TAYLORGREEN
    ... with incomp_stress='moment'

As in namelist/incomp_blowup3d.nml the two rungs differ in ONE entry,
incomp_stress, and share dfmm_tau, so they differ in the closure alone.
Do not write an ampersand-prefixed group name anywhere in this header:
gfortran's namelist reader treats it as the start of that group and then fails
on the surrounding prose.

WHY THIS PROBLEM.  With k = 2 pi/boxlen,

    u = ( sin(kx) cos(ky), -cos(kx) sin(ky), 0 ) * exp(-2 nu k^2 t)

is an EXACT solution of incompressible Navier-Stokes.  It is
divergence-free, and its nonlinear term (u.grad)u is a pure gradient, so the
pressure absorbs the advection entirely and the solution is pure viscous
decay at a rate set by nothing but nu k^2.  That makes it a simultaneous test
of the viscous term, the Leray projection and the time integrator -- and,
because the SHAPE must not distort while the amplitude decays, a sharp test
that the advection scheme recognises a pure gradient.  u_z must stay
identically zero and rho must stay uniform to the bit.

GATE 4 (rung 1).  nu = incomp_p0*dfmm_tau/incomp_rho0, so the values below
(dfmm_tau = 0.02, p0 = rho0 = 1) give nu = 0.02 and

    max|u|(t=0.1) / max|u|(0) = exp(-2 nu k^2 t) = 0.8539234976

with 2 nu k^2 = 1.5791367 and a viscous time 1/(2 nu k^2) = 0.6333, i.e.
t = 0.1 is 0.158 viscous times -- long enough to be a real test of the
dissipation rate, short enough that the amplitude has not decayed into
round-off.  Measured at level 5 (32^3): max|u| ratio 1.0000007,
max|u - u_exact| 6.0e-7, u_z identically zero, div u 1.3e-15.  The standalone
solver gives 2.3e-5 / 5.3e-6 / 6.8e-7 at N = 8/16/32, which is the RK2 rate at
dt ~ dx.

GATE 6 (rung 4).  Rebuild at DFMM=4, set incomp_stress='moment', and run the
same problem at dfmm_tau = 0.02, 0.002, 0.0005.  Rung 4 must reduce to rung 1
as tau -> 0, since nu = p_0 tau/rho_0 is the Chapman-Enskog viscosity of the
evolved stress.  The analytic rung-1 answers at t = 0.1 are

    dfmm_tau    nu        max|u|/max|u|(0)     measured ratio    max|Pi|/p_0
    -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    0.02        0.02        0.8539234976          1.0295           0.243
    0.002       0.002       0.9843326629          1.00075          0.0247
    0.0005      0.0005      0.9960599407          1.000166         0.0062

where "measured ratio" is rung 4's amplitude divided by the analytic value in
the column to its left.  The convergence is first order in tau, as it must be.
The 3% excess at tau = 0.02 is NOT an error: the evolved stress lags the
strain, so it dissipates LESS than the Newtonian stress does, and the flow
decays more slowly.  That lag is the whole reason the moment rungs exist.  Use
tau = 0.0005 as the regression value; use tau = 0.02 to see the lag.

GATE 8 (rung 4, the Burnett heat flux).  Q_ijk IS carried at DFMM>=2, and the
same three runs give a second, sharper gate.  theta = p_0/rho_0 is constant
here, so the FIRST-ORDER Chapman-Enskog heat flux vanishes identically -- but
Q's production is -theta_0 (d_k Pi_ij + d_j Pi_ik + d_i Pi_jk) + O(Pi^2),
which is nonzero wherever Pi varies.  Contracting gives

    q_i -> -tau_q theta_0 d_j Pi_ij  =  2 tau_q theta_0 p_0 tau k^2 * (the
                                        Taylor-Green shape) ,  tau_q = tau/Pr

a Burnett-order heat flux driven by the velocity Laplacian.  With Pr = 2/3 and
the discrete max of |sin kx cos ky| on 32^3 equal to 0.9903926402, the
prediction and the measurement are

    dfmm_tau    max|q| predicted   measured     ratio
    ----------------------------------------------------
    0.02          4.007e-02        5.776e-02    1.4415
    0.002         4.618e-04        4.675e-04    1.0122
    0.0005        2.921e-05        2.932e-05    1.0036

converging to 1 as a second-order prediction must, and failing by 44% at
tau = 0.02 where max|Pi|/p_0 = 0.21 and it should fail.  The tau^2 scaling
holds to 0.8% between the two small-tau points.  Because the first-order value
is EXACTLY zero, this gate tests Q's production with no leading-order term to
hide behind -- including the exact -d_l R + T_Q1 cancellation, which is
analytic here and only discrete in the compressible code.

A DFMM=1 build with incomp_stress='moment' gives the TEN-moment incompressible
rung instead (Pi only).  That is a legitimate object and the code says which
one is running at startup, but it is NOT comparable with a DFMM>=2
compressible run.  Note the twenty-moment rung is 3.4x more expensive at the
same tau (81 steps vs 24 here): once Q is carried, the Pi <-> Q pair is
hyperbolic at the thermal speed and incomp_dt has a limb for it.

TWO THINGS THAT WILL MISLEAD YOU IF YOU READ THE LOG NAIVELY.
  * The 'incomp level=' diagnostic prints the state AFTER the step whose
    header line precedes it, so the first one is already one step old.  The
    initial max|u| is not in the log at all.  On a 32^3 collocated grid the
    discrete maximum of |u| is 0.9904392375, not 1, because no cell centre
    lands on the continuum extremum -- normalise by that, or better, compare
    against the analytic field sampled on the SAME cell centres, which is what
    the numbers above do.  The log prints four significant digits, so it
    resolves the ratio only to ~1e-4; the 6e-7 above comes from the snapshot.
  * dfmm_tau lives in both DFMM_PARAMS and INCOMP_PARAMS, and read_params
    reads INCOMP_PARAMS second (amr/read_params.f90:1063), so the
    INCOMP_PARAMS value wins.  Set it in one group only.

WHAT TO WATCH IN THE LOG.
  * div u every step.  It must stay at 1e-15.  Anything larger is a bug, not
    a tolerance.
  * mcons exactly zero.  econs is MEANINGLESS in these rungs: eint is pinned
    at 3 p_0/2, so dissipated kinetic energy leaves the budget instead of
    heating the gas.
  * E_trunc/E, the energy in the band the 2/3 dealiasing removes.  1.7e-16 for
    rung 1 here; it is the diagnostic that announces under-resolution first.

RESOLUTION.  levelmin must equal levelmax and the grid must be a power of
two; anything else is rejected at startup by incomp_validate.  The solver is
host-side float64, so cost scales as the FFT: level 5 (32^3) is ~0.2 s/step.

EXACT OUTPUT TIME.  tout_exact=.true. clips the coarse timestep so the
snapshot lands ON tout instead of on the first step past it.  It matters here
because the deliverable is an amplitude AT a time compared against
exp(-2 nu k^2 t): a 0.9% overshoot in t is a 1.4% error in the reference
value, which would swamp the 6e-7 the solver actually achieves.  With it the
landing is exact to 2e-14 relative.

&RUN_PARAMS
hydro=.true.
ncontrol=1
nrestart=0
nremap=0
nsubcycle=10*1
verbose=.false.
nstepmax=100000
nsuperoct=2
/

&AMR_PARAMS
levelmin=5
levelmax=5
ngridtot=6000
ncachemax=3000
nexpand=1
boxlen=1.0
/

&INIT_PARAMS
nregion=1
region_type(1)='square'
x_center=0.5
y_center=0.5
z_center=0.5
length_x=10.0
length_y=10.0
length_z=10.0
exp_region=10.0
d_region=1.0
u_region=0.0
v_region=0.0
w_region=0.0
p_region=1.0
/

&OUTPUT_PARAMS
foutput=1000000   ! >0 so the tout-driven snapshot fires (amr_step.f90:107)
tout=0.1
tout_exact=.true. ! land the snapshot ON tout; the answer is a value AT a time
/

&HYDRO_PARAMS
gamma=1.6666666666666667
courant_factor=0.4
slope_type=1
riemann='hll'
/

&INCOMP_PARAMS
incompressible=.true.
incomp_p0=1.0
incomp_rho0=1.0
incomp_stress='viscous'   ! rung 1; set 'moment' for rung 4 (needs DFMM=4)
incomp_diag=.true.
dfmm_tau=0.02             ! nu = incomp_p0*dfmm_tau/incomp_rho0
/

&DFMM_PARAMS
dfmm_prandtl=0.6666666666666667
dfmm_source=.true.
dfmm_diag=.false.
dfmm_ic_sigmax=0.02
/
